CORE dfcc-only
main.c
--dfcc main --apply-loop-contracts _ --no-standard-checks
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^\[main.assertion.\d+\] .* x counts the iterations: SUCCESS$
^\[main.assertion.\d+\] .* g written as a global: FAILURE$
^\[main.assertion.\d+\] .* y written through a parameter: FAILURE$
^\[main.assertion.\d+\] .* x not written by the second loop: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^\[.*\.assigns\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
^\[main\.loop_assigns\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
^\[main\.loop_invariant_(base|step)\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
--
This test checks that the second loop still havocs its inferred targets g and
y when the first loop has an explicit assigns clause. The writes happen in a
later iteration, so the assertions after the loop fail only if it havocs them.
